<!--
beet's own entry: the dev commands, wired as routes over the capabilities linked
into the `beet` binary.

The wasm test runner depends on it: `.cargo/config.toml` sets
`runner = 'beet run-wasm'`, so `cargo test --target wasm32-...` invokes
`beet run-wasm <module>`, which discovers this entry and dispatches the
`<RunWasm/>` route. The rest are convenience: serve, build, check, static export,
pdf, s3 sync.

It is a one-shot `<CliServer{always:true}>` root with the `Router` as its child: the
binary injects nothing, the server's `CallOnLoad` fires the process request on load,
it hops down to the router, the positional route arg selects the command, and the
command resolves and the process exits. `always=true` makes this dispatcher run on every boot
regardless of `--server`, since `--server` selects the *served site's* servers, not
this entry's. The one exception to exiting is `<Serve/>`, which loads a site and
boots *its* servers through a direct boot call on the loaded root, parking so the
process serves.

    cargo install --path crates/beet-cli --all-features   # then, from anywhere in the repo:
    beet run-wasm <module.wasm>            # run a wasm test module
    beet check examples/bsx_site           # lint a no-code site
    beet export-static examples/bsx_site   # write its dist/
    just build-wasm-min                    # the smallest browser runtime (assets/wasm/beet-min.wasm)
    just build-wasm-full                   # ..and the kitchen sink (assets/wasm/beet-full.wasm)

The two sites in this repo both serve through `<Serve/>` (`<site>` is a directory
holding an entry document, or the entry file itself):

    beet serve site                        # the beet website (site/main.bsx), http
    beet serve examples/bsx_site           # a zero-code BSX site, http
    beet serve site/main.bsx               # ..the entry file resolves the same site
    beet serve site --server=tui           # ..as a live terminal (like `beet present`)
    beet serve site --server=http,ssh      # ..http plus a multi-tenant ssh terminal

It also deploys the beet website to AWS Fargate (the site served from S3, html
over http + a live ssh terminal), fronted by Cloudflare's edge cache: http
hostnames are proxied and edge-cached, ssh (and future live apps) ride the
DNS-only `app.beet.org` / `app.dev.beet.org` hostnames straight to the NLB,
`<CloudflareZoneSetup/>` converges the zone-level edge config (cache ruleset,
strict TLS), and every deploy or sync ends with a `<CloudflarePurgeCache/>` so
content changes are live at the edge immediately. On the deploy route the purge
comes *after* `<FargateWatch/>`, since purging while the old tasks still serve
just re-caches the old responses for the full edge TTL.

The deploy route applies once per layer, and the order is the point: `<TofuApply
layer="storage"/>` brings up the stores (the app bucket, the image registry, the
analytics table: the addresses their blocks declare under the `storage` layer,
an overridable per-block field), `<BuildDockerImage/>` and `<DirSync/>` fill
them, and a bare `<TofuApply/>` converges the whole stack, rolling a task
definition onto an image that is pushed and a bucket that already holds the
site. One apply rolls the new task first, so it boots against an empty bucket,
finds no entry document and exits. Naming a layer no block declares is a loud
error, never a silent skip. The ECS deployment circuit breaker rides on that
ordering: with the sync ahead of the roll, a task that still fails to boot is a
genuinely broken image, so the deployment fails and restores the previous task
definition.

The deploy routes hang off a stack-bearing `<BeetSiteDeployHost>` so they resolve
a `Stack` by ancestry without a second server. `--stage=prod` targets production
(apex `beet.org` + `www`); the default `dev` stage publishes only `dev.beet.org`.

The assets bucket (`beet-site--shared--assets`, the source of record for files
too large for git) lives on its own `shared` stage: a second `<BeetSiteDeployHost
stage="shared">` under the `shared/` route prefix, provisioned as its own step
since a source shared by developers is a different process from a stage deploy.
No stage deploy or destroy touches it, and no deployed binary reads it: a stage
deploy replicates the checkout (`site/assets` is a committed symlink to
`./assets`, and the sync follows it), so the assets reach an app bucket the same
way every other file does.

    just beet-deploy [--stage=prod]        # build -> stores -> image -> sync -> roll -> watch -> purge
    just beet-sync                         # re-publish the site (no image rebuild)
    just beet-watch                        # poll the rollout
    just beet-shared apply                 # provision the shared assets bucket
    just beet-shared pull                  # hydrate ./assets from it (no credentials)
    just beet-shared push                  # publish ./assets to it

Live deploy needs AWS credentials + `BEET_SSH_HOST_KEY`/`CLOUDFLARE_ZONE_ID`/
`CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN` in `.env` (see `.env.example` for the token permissions),
plus `tofu`, `cargo-zigbuild` and a container engine.
-->
<StartOnLoad {CliServer{always:true}}>
<Router {HelpHandler}>
	<Serve/>
	<RunWasm/>
	<BuildWasm/>
	<Check/>
	<AnalyticsReport/>
	<ExportStatic/>
	<ExportPdf/>
	<CaptureScreenshot/>
	<SyncS3/>
	<BeetSiteDeployHost>
		<Route path="deploy" {ExchangeSequence}>
			<FargateBeetSiteBlock app_name="beet-site"/>
			<AppBucket/>
			<AnalyticsTable/>
			<BeetBinaryBuild features="aws_sdk,ssh,geoip"/>
			<TofuApply layer="storage"/>
			<BuildDockerImage/>
			<DirSync app_name="beet-site" bucket="app" local_dir="site" follow_symlinks=true delete=true/>
			<TofuApply/>
			<CloudflareZoneSetup/>
			<FargateWatch app_name="beet-site" timeout="300s"/>
			<CloudflarePurgeCache/>
		</Route>
		<Route path="sync" {ExchangeSequence}>
			<DirSync app_name="beet-site" bucket="app" local_dir="site" follow_symlinks=true delete=true/>
			<CloudflarePurgeCache/>
		</Route>
		<Route path="watch" {ExchangeSequence}>
			<FargateWatch app_name="beet-site" timeout="300s"/>
		</Route>
	</BeetSiteDeployHost>
	<Route path="shared">
		<BeetSiteDeployHost stage="shared">
			<AssetsBucket/>
			<Route path="push" {ExchangeSequence}>
				<DirSync app_name="beet-site" bucket="assets" local_dir="assets" stage="shared"/>
			</Route>
			<Route path="pull" {ExchangeSequence}>
				<DirSync app_name="beet-site" bucket="assets" local_dir="assets" stage="shared" direction="Pull" no_sign_request=true/>
			</Route>
		</BeetSiteDeployHost>
	</Route>
</Router>
</StartOnLoad>
